    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Field investigations revealed five characteristic deformation bands patterns–parallel, anastamozing parallel, conjugate, sigmoidal, and boxwork–in the Aztec sandstone in the Valley of Fire State Park, Nevada. The patterns are shown to be continuous on length scales ranging from 1 m to >100 m. Deformation bands within the patterns vary in thickness from 1 mm to 1.5 cm, and the spacing between bands varies from ~1mm to 2 m. Conceptual models for the formations of these patterns are presented. Analytical methods and fine-scale finite difference and finite element modeling based on homogenization theory and capable of discretely representing the deformation bands are then used to calculate upscaled effective permeability tensors for the matrix rock plus the deformation bands. Effective permeability tensors are calculated for each of the deformation band patterns while varying the band permeability/matrix permeability ratio and while varying the thickness and spacing of the deformation bands. It is found that each of the patterns significantly effects the rock permeability and creates permeability anisotropy. The permeability reductions and anisotopries are of the same order of magnitude as those caused by the presence of shale lenses within sandstone. Small-Scale heterogeneities can have equally important effects on permeability and permeability anisotropies as large-scale heterogeneities. An understanding of the effects of the deformation band patterns on the bulk permeability and permeability anisotropy of sandstone can be used to optimize petroleum recovery strategies.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
